    "growth_divergence_warning": "Equity 3yr CAGR (12.9%) diverges >30% from revenue 3yr (-1.3%): book value rebuilt from the FY2022 trough because buybacks were halted (FY2023 $0) and only partly resumed, not because the business grew. Equity 5yr (2.3%) is the conservative check and agrees with revenue 5yr, which is selected.",
    "notes": "Periods are Target fiscal years (CSV labels one year ahead). Owner-FCF series = reported FCF - SBC - equity-award taxes: FY2020 ~$7,666M (pandemic peak), FY2022 -$1,914M (inventory glut + $5.5B capex, so 3yr CAGR undefined), FY2023 $3,433M, FY2024 $4,070M, FY2025 $2,486M. The 5yr owner-FCF CAGR (-20.2%) is below reported (-18.5%) because SBC+withholding has risen from ~$210M to ~$349M while FCF fell; both start at a COVID-windfall base and say more about FY2020 than about the trend. EPS 3yr CAGR runs off the FY2022 inventory-markdown trough."
  },
  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "Fiddelke's plan delivers the guided ~5% FY2026 sales growth (H1 +6%, Q2 comp +3.8% with traffic +3.6%), then growth settles at ~3% as the boycott comparisons lapse: low-single-digit comps plus ~1pt from 30+ new stores a year. Roundel and Target Circle 360 add high-margin other revenue, and the operating margin climbs back to ~5.6% (pre-SBC EBITDA 9.0%) - better than the FY2023-25 ~5% underlying but short of the FY2018-19 6%+, because Walmart and Costco keep the price gap (~5% above Walmart) from narrowing on Target's terms. The $994M tariff refund is treated as a one-off and is in no forward year. The ~$5B capex year is a step, not a new run-rate: capex glides to 3.4% of sales.",
    "bull": "The Enterprise Acceleration Office turns the reset into sustained share gains: traffic stays positive, owned brands and the Home/Baby/seasonal refresh restore 'Tar-zhay' discovery, Roundel doubles as management targets and Circle 360 membership compounds, lifting mix. Sales grow 6% in FY2026 and 3.5-4% for several years as the 300-store decade plan executes; the operating margin returns to ~6.4%, matching FY2018-19, and the business earns a slightly higher exit multiple.",
    "bear": "The FY2026 recovery proves to be easy comparisons plus a tariff windfall. Grocery and essentials trips continue to migrate to Walmart (20-27% grocery share), Costco and Amazon/Whole Foods; Target's discretionary-heavy mix (apparel, home, electronics) meets a softer middle-income consumer and renewed tariff costs; reputational exposure from both sides of the DEI fight flares again. Sales grow ~1-2% after FY2026, the margin stays at the FY2023-25 underlying level, and the heavy capex programme earns little - management cuts it back. Investment-grade balance sheet means this is a value bear, not a solvency bear."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "A mature US mass-merchant valued on owner FCF with the FY2026 tariff refund and the FY2025 litigation gain stripped from the margin base. The question the price asks is whether Target's operating margin returns to its pre-COVID ~6%: the base case assumes most, not all, of the way back.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Operating-margin path: each 50bp of mature pre-SBC EBITDA margin is worth roughly $10/share",
      "Grocery/essentials share loss to Walmart, Costco and Amazon reducing trip frequency",
      "Tariff policy: the FY2026 refund shows how large tariff swings are relative to a ~5% operating margin",
      "Return on the ~$5B capex step-up (new stores, 130+ remodels) - capex is held above D&A for a decade in every case",
      "Brand/political exposure (2025 DEI boycott) and CEO-transition execution",
      "Discretionary-heavy category mix in a consumer slowdown"
    ]
  },
